00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  os login is a compute engine offering  that provides a way to manage ssh access  to linux virtual machine instances using  iem roles  when os login is enabled a linux user  account is directly tied to a user's  google identity  this means that a user has a specific  username and uid in all os login vms in  their project organization  with os login you can also restrict root  access  you can enable os login for individual  vms or for all vms in your project to  enable os login for a specific vm go to  the compute engine vm instances page and  select your instance  start editing the instance and navigate  to the custom metadata section  you want to add the key enabled os login  and set the value to true then click  save  os login is now enabled on this instance  and only users with the appropriate iem  roles can connect to the vm using ssh  alternatively you can enable os login  for all the instances in your project by  navigating to the compute engine  metadata page and adding enable dash os  login with the value  true once this metadata key is added os  login is enabled on all linux vms in the  project you can disable those login on a  specific instance by adding the metadata  enable os login to false at the instance  level  you can also enable os login when  creating a vm in the advanced security  options we will go over how to do this  in a later example to grant a user  permission to connect to vms that have  os log enabled the user must have the  required os login iam rules  let's take a closer look at these roles  compute os admin login  allows the user to ssh into the instance  and run commands as an administrator  while computeros login allows them to  ssh in without administrative access  the iim service account user role allows  a user to run operations as a service  account this is a necessary permission  to log into a vm that is associated with  the service account you can grant the  compute os login roles at the instance  level or at the project level so they  apply to all resources inside your  project  the service account user role can be  granted on the project or on individual  service accounts in scenarios where  users outside your organization require  access to your vms your organization  administrator must grant the compute os  log an external user role to those users  at the organization level  let's run through an example  we will start by creating a compute  engine instance we are going to leave  all the fields as default scroll down  and expand to the security section here  you can see the manage access section  and the option control vm access to iem  permissions  this will ensure that our newly created  vm will have os login enabled  let's attempt to ssh into the vm as a  user without the compute admin role  and is unsuccessful  this is expected because we have not yet  given the necessary permissions to this  user account  let's go ahead and grant the appropriate  iem roles to my user account  in the scenario we would like this user  to be able to ssh into the vm but not  run any commands as the root account  therefore we need to grant this user the  compute os login role  now let's try to ssh once again  wait a few seconds  and we are able to ssh into the vm  successfully  in summary os login can simplify ssh  access management your compute engine  instances by using iam roles when  granting these roles always keep in mind  the security best practice of lease  privilege and set your permissions  accordingly  [Music]

